## 5. SPECTRAL LOGIC

### 5.1 Spectral Connectives

**Definition 5.1 (Spectral Connectives)**
For x, y ∈ [0,1]:

| Connective | Definition | Interpretation |
|:---|:---|:---|
| **Negation** | ¬ₛ x = 1 - x | Spectral not |
| **Conjunction** | x ∧ₛ y = min(x, y) | Spectral and |
| **Disjunction** | x ∨ₛ y = max(x, y) | Spectral or |
| **Implication** | x →ₛ y = 1 if x ≤ y, else 1 - (x - y) | Spectral implies |

**Theorem 5.1 (Spectral Logic Properties)**
The spectral connectives satisfy:

| Property | Classical | Spectral |
|:---|:---|:---|
| Commutativity | x ∧ y = y ∧ x | min(x,y) = min(y,x) ✓ |
| Associativity | (x ∧ y) ∧ z = x ∧ (y ∧ z) | ✓ |
| Distributivity | x ∧ (y ∨ z) = (x ∧ y) ∨ (x ∧ z) | ✓ |
| Complement | x ∧ ¬x = 0 | x ∧ₛ ¬ₛ x ≤ 0.5 (not 0) |
| Double Negation | ¬¬x = x | ¬ₛ¬ₛx = x ✓ |

*Proof:* All follow from properties of min, max, and 1-x.

### 5.2 Spectral Quantifiers

**Definition 5.2 (Spectral Universal)**
For a predicate P: 𝕊 → [0,1]:
```
∀ₛ x ∈ 𝕊, P(x) = inf{P(x) | x ∈ 𝕊}
```

**Definition 5.3 (Spectral Existential)**
For a predicate P: 𝕊 → [0,1]:
```
∃ₛ x ∈ 𝕊, P(x) = sup{P(x) | x ∈ 𝕊}
```

**Theorem 5.2 (Quantifier Duality)**
```
¬ₛ(∀ₛ x, P(x)) = ∃ₛ x, ¬ₛP(x)
```
*Proof:* 1 - inf P(x) = sup (1 - P(x)).

